WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.660
So, I want you to imagine for a second that you are managing this massive construction crew, right?

00:05.720 --> 00:07.400
Like, you're building a massive skyscraper.

00:07.520 --> 00:07.980
Oh, man.

00:08.400 --> 00:09.780
That sounds stressful already.

00:09.940 --> 00:11.120
Right. And it gets worse.

00:11.900 --> 00:17.260
Your framers are over in Tokyo, the concrete-pouring teams are in New York,

00:17.380 --> 00:19.840
and your electricians are, I don't know, sitting in London.

00:20.060 --> 00:21.280
Yeah, completely scattered.

00:21.460 --> 00:22.100
Completely scattered.

00:22.520 --> 00:24.960
And they all have, you know, slightly different blueprints.

00:25.560 --> 00:30.060
Plus, by law, you can only actually communicate with them exactly once a month.

00:30.520 --> 00:33.180
Wow. That is a recipe for disaster.

00:33.580 --> 00:34.500
It really is.

00:34.500 --> 00:38.360
I mean, the result is obviously a lopsided tower.

00:38.680 --> 00:43.400
You've got wires leading to absolutely nowhere and just a fundamentally unstable structure.

00:43.940 --> 00:46.680
Which, yeah, no one would ever want to step foot in that building.

00:46.920 --> 00:47.300
Exactly.

00:47.860 --> 00:51.960
But the thing is, this isn't just some wacky construction hypothetical.

00:51.960 --> 00:57.780
It is, it's the exact nightmare that's playing out right now in the multi-billion dollar race

00:57.780 --> 01:00.040
to train global artificial intelligence models.

01:00.540 --> 01:01.220
It really is.

01:01.460 --> 01:04.280
Because the structural integrity of these AI models,

01:04.460 --> 01:09.240
it relies entirely on these independent, geographically isolated machines.

01:09.420 --> 01:13.840
And they are all trying to, like, agree on a single mathematical reality.

01:14.080 --> 01:16.000
While dealing with huge bottlenecks, right?

01:16.140 --> 01:18.220
Oh, massive communication bottlenecks, yeah.

01:18.520 --> 01:19.580
Well, welcome to this deep dive.

01:19.580 --> 01:24.060
Today, we are unpacking a really dense, just highly mathematical paper

01:24.060 --> 01:25.960
by an independent researcher named Flyxion.

01:26.440 --> 01:29.300
Yeah, it's called Hierarchical Synchronization.

01:29.860 --> 01:32.900
Local Training, Global State, and the Composition of Updates.

01:33.080 --> 01:34.720
That's a mouthful of a title for sure.

01:34.900 --> 01:35.360
It is.

01:35.460 --> 01:37.120
But the paper itself is brilliant.

01:37.400 --> 01:42.560
It basically dissects the mechanics of how distributed systems actually reach consensus.

01:43.020 --> 01:43.280
Right.

01:43.280 --> 01:47.600
Like, it moves way past the surface-level engineering of, you know, network bandwidth.

01:47.960 --> 01:53.040
And it really digs into the fundamental structural mathematics of how we combine all these disparate

01:53.040 --> 01:54.440
updates from isolated computers.

01:55.180 --> 01:55.220
Right.

01:55.300 --> 01:57.420
Which brings us to our mission for this deep dive today.

01:57.600 --> 02:02.380
We really want to explore how these independent remote training sites, these computers,

02:02.560 --> 02:07.600
can work together to build a shared AI model without, you know, drowning in the sheer cost

02:07.600 --> 02:09.520
of saying terabytes of data back and forth.

02:09.620 --> 02:11.900
Because that data transfer is incredibly expensive.

02:11.900 --> 02:13.020
Super expensive.

02:13.360 --> 02:17.180
But even more importantly, we're going to uncover the mathematical traps that are kind

02:17.180 --> 02:18.500
of baked into this whole process.

02:18.740 --> 02:18.880
Yeah.

02:18.980 --> 02:20.420
The traps are the really scary part.

02:20.680 --> 02:21.320
They really are.

02:21.780 --> 02:26.920
Traps that can completely ruin a model even if, say, the network hardware itself works flawlessly.

02:27.200 --> 02:27.760
So, okay.

02:28.220 --> 02:28.920
Let's unpack this.

02:29.240 --> 02:29.440
Yeah.

02:29.540 --> 02:33.340
To really grasp this, we have to kind of shift our perspective.

02:33.820 --> 02:39.400
We need to zoom out from the macro level of the giant AI and go down to the local reality

02:39.400 --> 02:41.180
of the individual training sites.

02:41.180 --> 02:41.460
Okay.

02:41.540 --> 02:43.200
So, it boots on the ground at the local server.

02:43.460 --> 02:43.940
Exactly.

02:44.460 --> 02:50.080
So, in a decentralized training environment, you have this central server and it periodically

02:50.080 --> 02:51.580
broadcasts a checkpoint.

02:51.920 --> 02:53.360
And what's in that checkpoint exactly?

02:53.880 --> 02:58.120
Basically, the checkpoint contains the entirety of the current global model.

02:58.240 --> 03:01.380
So, all the weights, the biases, the optimizer states.

03:01.800 --> 03:06.560
It's the baseline reality for the entire network at that specific moment in time.

03:06.560 --> 03:07.080
Got it.

03:07.420 --> 03:12.420
So, all the local sites receive that checkpoint and then the communication lines just essentially

03:12.420 --> 03:13.220
go dead, right?

03:13.380 --> 03:13.580
Right.

03:13.660 --> 03:14.800
They go completely dark.

03:15.020 --> 03:19.200
So, the sites then train the model locally using their own isolated data sets.

03:19.560 --> 03:23.280
And then after a set number of local steps, they generate a shift.

03:23.700 --> 03:24.760
A shift, exactly.

03:25.140 --> 03:26.100
Which is just the delta.

03:26.420 --> 03:26.640
Right.

03:26.760 --> 03:27.160
The delta.

03:27.160 --> 03:31.860
It's the mathematical representation of the changes they've made to the weights based on

03:31.860 --> 03:32.720
their own local data.

03:33.220 --> 03:36.120
And then they send that shift back into the network to be aggregated.

03:36.280 --> 03:36.400
Yeah.

03:36.440 --> 03:39.900
And the paper points out this fundamental paradox in the architecture right away.

03:40.420 --> 03:45.120
Flyxion writes, the assumption of a global state is compatible with local ignorance.

03:45.240 --> 03:45.940
Local ignorance.

03:45.940 --> 03:47.340
I love that phrasing.

03:47.760 --> 03:48.540
It's very accurate.

03:48.800 --> 03:48.980
Yeah.

03:49.120 --> 03:53.440
Because during an active training round, the nodes in the network are operating with entirely

03:53.440 --> 03:54.600
fragmented knowledge.

03:54.760 --> 03:58.460
So, if you picture the whole network as a giant branching tree, right?

03:58.760 --> 04:03.680
Like a relay node sitting somewhere in the middle, it might know that a specific cluster

04:03.680 --> 04:07.060
of leaf nodes below it has finished processing.

04:07.700 --> 04:08.160
Exactly.

04:08.340 --> 04:08.480
Yeah.

04:08.480 --> 04:09.640
It sees its own little world.

04:09.640 --> 04:14.700
But it has absolutely no idea if a node on an entirely different branch has even, you

04:14.700 --> 04:16.560
know, downloaded the initial checkpoint yet.

04:16.900 --> 04:16.980
Right.

04:17.080 --> 04:19.880
A relay only has visibility into its own subtree.

04:20.280 --> 04:24.480
And this fragmented visibility, it forces the system to answer three entirely distinct

04:24.480 --> 04:27.580
questions at every single synchronization boundary.

04:27.820 --> 04:28.020
Okay.

04:28.100 --> 04:28.920
Lay those out for us.

04:29.240 --> 04:29.460
Sure.

04:29.600 --> 04:30.360
So, question one.

04:30.680 --> 04:34.160
Do the local records agree on what actually happened in the network?

04:34.280 --> 04:35.020
The basic facts.

04:35.360 --> 04:35.500
Yeah.

04:35.500 --> 04:40.580
Question two, how do we mathematically combine the distinct updates submitted by all those

04:40.580 --> 04:40.860
sites?

04:41.120 --> 04:41.620
Doing the math.

04:41.960 --> 04:42.080
Yep.

04:42.580 --> 04:48.240
And question three, does the resulting combined update actually advance the intended objective

04:48.240 --> 04:49.380
of the AI model?

04:49.860 --> 04:50.120
Okay.

04:50.220 --> 04:53.480
So, think about it like organizing a massive global potluck.

04:53.860 --> 04:54.680
Oh, I like this.

04:54.780 --> 04:55.300
It's a potluck.

04:55.460 --> 04:55.560
Yeah.

04:55.620 --> 04:57.640
So, question one is just the logistics, right?

04:57.740 --> 05:01.020
Like, did everyone agree on the recipe and who is actually bringing what?

05:01.320 --> 05:01.520
Okay.

05:01.720 --> 05:01.860
Yeah.

05:02.240 --> 05:03.900
Then question two, it's the execution.

05:03.900 --> 05:08.400
Did we mix all the ingredients correctly without, you know, spilling anything or double

05:08.400 --> 05:09.120
measuring the flour?

05:09.400 --> 05:10.220
Which is the math.

05:10.440 --> 05:10.720
Exactly.

05:11.400 --> 05:12.980
And then question three is the outcome.

05:13.360 --> 05:15.300
Does the final dish actually taste good?

05:15.600 --> 05:15.940
Right.

05:16.400 --> 05:21.020
And if we connect this to the bigger picture, the real risk of decentralized systems is that

05:21.020 --> 05:25.000
an affirmative answer to one of those questions leaves the other two completely open.

05:25.220 --> 05:25.660
Oh, interesting.

05:26.100 --> 05:27.820
So, they aren't guaranteed to follow each other.

05:27.980 --> 05:28.620
Not at all.

05:29.080 --> 05:33.560
You can have a flawlessly logged network where everyone completely agrees on the recipe.

05:34.260 --> 05:38.860
But if the mathematical aggregation is flawed, well, you've mixed the ingredients wrong.

05:38.980 --> 05:39.780
The cake is ruined.

05:39.940 --> 05:40.960
The cake is totally ruined.

05:41.340 --> 05:46.060
Or alternatively, you can perfectly mix the ingredients according to the recipe, but the

05:46.060 --> 05:51.960
model still degrades because those local ingredients just don't combine to form a coherent global

05:51.960 --> 05:52.380
dish.

05:52.380 --> 05:53.420
Man, that is tricky.

05:53.420 --> 05:58.120
So, the first major trap we really need to look at is that disconnect between the first

05:58.120 --> 05:59.060
two questions, right?

05:59.540 --> 06:03.340
The difference between keeping the record and actually doing the math.

06:03.700 --> 06:03.800
Yeah.

06:03.960 --> 06:04.560
This is crucial.

06:05.000 --> 06:09.360
Flyxion introduces two concepts to frame how a distributed system logs its reality.

06:09.600 --> 06:11.400
The first is the sheaf condition.

06:11.640 --> 06:12.420
The sheaf condition.

06:12.420 --> 06:12.740
Right.

06:12.940 --> 06:17.240
It basically dictates exact agreement on overlapping data.

06:17.900 --> 06:23.020
So, if two different observing nodes both log an event, let's say WorkerA, submitting

06:23.020 --> 06:28.080
a specific update, their records have to match down to the cryptographic hash.

06:28.340 --> 06:29.620
So, no discrepancies at all?

06:29.760 --> 06:29.960
Zero.

06:30.440 --> 06:36.000
The base checkpoint used, the schema, the payload digest, all of it must be identical.

06:36.380 --> 06:39.840
Basically, if they overlap, they have to glue together perfectly without any contradiction.

06:39.960 --> 06:40.280
Exactly.

06:40.720 --> 06:45.760
And then the second concept is the information order, which deals with accumulating incomplete

06:45.760 --> 06:46.800
evidence over time.

06:46.980 --> 06:47.240
Okay.

06:47.380 --> 06:47.960
How does that work?

06:48.440 --> 06:54.000
Well, if my logs show that WorkerA and WorkerB submitted their shifts, and your logs show

06:54.000 --> 06:58.320
that WorkerB and WorkerC submitted theirs, we can safely join that knowledge.

06:58.660 --> 06:58.960
Oh, I see.

06:58.960 --> 07:00.520
So, we've just merged them.

07:01.100 --> 07:01.160
Right.

07:01.300 --> 07:03.480
We now know that A, B, and C have all participated.

07:03.820 --> 07:04.000
Okay.

07:04.180 --> 07:09.720
So, merging that knowledge, it introduces this really vital property of the historical record,

07:09.840 --> 07:11.540
which is idempotence.

07:11.960 --> 07:12.400
Yes.

07:13.080 --> 07:13.600
Idempotence.

07:13.980 --> 07:15.620
A great computer science term.

07:15.820 --> 07:16.020
Yeah.

07:16.460 --> 07:22.740
So, in computer science, an operation is idempotent if applying it multiple times yields

07:22.740 --> 07:25.480
the exact same result as just applying it once.

07:25.760 --> 07:26.000
Right.

07:26.040 --> 07:28.040
Like pressing an elevator button a bunch of times.

07:28.040 --> 07:29.160
It doesn't make it come faster.

07:29.600 --> 07:29.820
Exactly.

07:30.560 --> 07:34.020
The historical record of a training ground operates this way.

07:34.420 --> 07:40.420
So, if two different relays report to the central server that WorkerA finished, the joint

07:40.420 --> 07:42.700
record simply states that WorkerA finished.

07:42.700 --> 07:43.260
Right.

07:43.260 --> 07:47.300
Repeating a factual observation doesn't alter the state of the record itself.

07:47.660 --> 07:52.180
The historical log is just recording the existence of the event, but the math being

07:52.180 --> 07:58.680
done on the payloads, like the actual numerical summaries of the AI weights, that is not idempotent.

07:58.680 --> 08:00.180
No, it is not.

08:00.320 --> 08:02.120
Summaries do not restrict themselves.

08:02.580 --> 08:02.800
Okay.

08:02.960 --> 08:07.140
So, break down the specific mathematical failure that the paper outlines here.

08:07.760 --> 08:08.000
Okay.

08:08.060 --> 08:11.000
Let's imagine a network with three active workers.

08:11.420 --> 08:14.440
Worker 1 and Worker 2 each have a data weight of 1.

08:14.860 --> 08:17.160
And Worker 3 has a weight of 2.

08:17.600 --> 08:17.880
Okay.

08:18.080 --> 08:18.740
Tracking so far.

08:18.740 --> 08:23.380
Now, they're computed shifts, which are the vectors they want to apply to the global model.

08:23.940 --> 08:29.180
Let's represent those by the values 2, negative 1, and 3, respectively.

08:29.740 --> 08:35.020
So, we have shifts of 2, negative 1, and 3 with varying weights.

08:35.140 --> 08:35.380
Right.

08:36.180 --> 08:41.040
Now, in a perfectly clean tree topology, aggregating these is super simple.

08:41.160 --> 08:42.200
You just add them up.

08:42.680 --> 08:44.760
But real-world networks are messy.

08:45.080 --> 08:45.700
They're chaotic.

08:46.100 --> 08:46.360
Very.

08:46.360 --> 08:53.660
They are often directed acyclic graphs, or DAGs, where routing paths can literally cross each other.

08:54.540 --> 08:59.860
So, suppose Relay Node 1 aggregates the outputs of Worker 1 and Worker 2.

09:00.340 --> 09:00.820
Okay.

09:00.960 --> 09:03.180
It calculates a weighted sum of their vectors.

09:03.940 --> 09:08.740
But meanwhile, Relay Node 2 aggregates the outputs of Worker 2 and Worker 3.

09:09.140 --> 09:12.380
Wait, so Worker 2's data just went to both relays?

09:13.060 --> 09:13.980
The routing overlapped?

09:14.360 --> 09:14.740
Exactly.

09:14.740 --> 09:19.620
Maybe for redundancy, or maybe load balancing, but it overlapped.

09:20.280 --> 09:24.740
Okay, so both relays then send their computed partial sums up to the central root server.

09:25.020 --> 09:25.380
Right.

09:26.120 --> 09:28.680
And the sheaf condition holds up perfectly here.

09:29.060 --> 09:33.300
Both relays agree exactly on the cryptographic identity of what Worker 2 submitted.

09:33.720 --> 09:35.340
So, the logs are totally pristine.

09:35.340 --> 09:36.920
The logs are flawless.

09:37.740 --> 09:48.300
But, if the root server relies on a standard operation-based aggregation, like just adding the numerical partial sums together, it mathematically double counts Worker 2.

09:48.620 --> 09:49.040
Hang on.

09:49.400 --> 09:51.340
I'm kind of stuck on this double counting thing.

09:51.500 --> 09:51.760
Okay.

09:51.940 --> 09:52.560
Where are you stuck?

09:53.020 --> 09:57.700
Well, if I hear a rumor from two different people, I don't believe the rumor twice as much.

09:57.700 --> 10:01.280
You know, I just realized two different people heard the exact same thing.

10:01.740 --> 10:03.460
My brain filters out the overlap.

10:03.860 --> 10:04.120
Right.

10:04.120 --> 10:09.680
So, why can't the network just look at the numerical data and realize, oh, hey, I already have this tensor.

10:10.000 --> 10:11.000
I shouldn't add it again.

10:11.660 --> 10:16.120
Because the root server isn't actually receiving the raw tensors from the workers anymore.

10:16.280 --> 10:16.860
Oh, it's not?

10:17.000 --> 10:20.080
No, it's receiving intermediate partial sums from the relays.

10:20.460 --> 10:25.180
So, it sees a combined vector from relay 1 and a combined vector from relay 2.

10:25.180 --> 10:31.980
It has literally no way of untangling those partial sums to see that Worker 2's data is secretly baked into both of them.

10:32.160 --> 10:32.880
Ah, I see.

10:33.400 --> 10:35.060
It's already mixed into the cake batter.

10:35.580 --> 10:36.060
Exactly.

10:36.460 --> 10:40.580
And even if it could see it, AI updates aren't qualitative facts like a rumor.

10:40.900 --> 10:44.860
They are geometric vectors in a multidimensional parameter space.

10:45.180 --> 10:49.620
So, the model is physically navigating coordinates to find, like, the lowest error rate.

10:49.780 --> 10:50.060
Right.

10:50.700 --> 10:52.580
An update vector is an instruction.

10:52.580 --> 10:57.580
It says, move 5 steps along this axis and 2 steps along this other axis.

10:58.360 --> 10:58.520
Okay.

10:58.860 --> 11:05.720
So, if you add a partial sum containing that instruction to another partial sum containing that exact same instruction,

11:06.100 --> 11:08.500
the system doesn't recognize the redundancy.

11:08.680 --> 11:10.200
It just executes the math.

11:10.440 --> 11:10.840
Oh, boy.

11:11.640 --> 11:13.680
So, you move 10 steps and 4 steps.

11:14.260 --> 11:14.780
Exactly.

11:15.500 --> 11:20.880
By double counting, you literally double the magnitude of that specific worker's gradient step.

11:20.880 --> 11:27.740
You're completely warping the global model's trajectory, yanking it toward one random node's local data set.

11:28.680 --> 11:38.760
So, the math just inherently acts on whatever is fed into it, meaning a perfectly functioning, highly redundant network topology is actively toxic to the AI's geometry.

11:39.120 --> 11:40.620
Toxic is a good word for it, yeah.

11:40.620 --> 11:43.500
How in the world does the paper propose to solve this?

11:43.800 --> 11:47.220
Well, Flyxion proposes the use of what they call tagged summaries.

11:47.440 --> 11:48.260
Tagged summaries.

11:48.460 --> 11:48.560
Okay.

11:48.700 --> 11:53.700
Basically, a numerical summary cannot just traverse the network as raw math anymore.

11:53.940 --> 11:59.360
It has to carry an exact, verifiable set of accepted contribution identifiers along with it.

11:59.360 --> 12:01.960
Oh, so it's like a payload attached to a guest list.

12:02.120 --> 12:03.460
A guest list, exactly.

12:04.040 --> 12:07.060
Like a bitmap or a bloom filter that explicitly states,

12:07.340 --> 12:10.920
hey, exactly this person's data is inside this partial sum.

12:11.440 --> 12:15.060
And so that changes the rules of aggregation at the relay level, right?

12:15.220 --> 12:15.660
Completely.

12:16.420 --> 12:22.640
A merge operation is strictly defined as valid only when the tag sets of the two summaries are entirely disjoint.

12:23.000 --> 12:24.200
No overlap allowed?

12:24.280 --> 12:24.660
None.

12:24.740 --> 12:29.980
If a parent node receives two summaries and sees that worker two's identifier is tagged in both,

12:30.480 --> 12:32.380
the merge is mathematically undefined.

12:32.740 --> 12:35.160
The system physically refuses to compute the addition.

12:35.580 --> 12:37.360
And just halt tree operation right there.

12:38.020 --> 12:39.100
So what happens next?

12:39.160 --> 12:43.020
Is it just like throw an error and crash the whole training round?

12:43.440 --> 12:43.800
No, no.

12:43.840 --> 12:45.560
It forces a resolution protocol.

12:46.020 --> 12:51.580
The parent node has to either recover the distinct leaf contributions from a lower level to bypass that overlap,

12:51.580 --> 12:56.860
or it must request a revised disjoint summary from one of its child relays.

12:57.220 --> 13:00.520
So it's enforcing strict structural accountability on the math.

13:00.640 --> 13:01.080
Exactly.

13:01.660 --> 13:07.140
Guaranteeing that the geometry of the update exactly matches that idempotent historical record we talked about.

13:07.280 --> 13:07.560
Okay.

13:08.580 --> 13:12.960
So tagged summaries solve the problem of nodes speaking twice.

13:13.160 --> 13:15.400
But what about the nodes that don't speak at all?

13:16.040 --> 13:16.960
The silent nodes.

13:17.400 --> 13:17.700
Right.

13:17.700 --> 13:22.040
If we are strictly regulating the math of the workers who do submit data,

13:22.840 --> 13:26.800
how does this distributed architecture handle a computer that just goes completely silent?

13:27.200 --> 13:29.360
This is a really cool part of the paper.

13:29.700 --> 13:33.280
It borrows a framework from asynchronous digital circuit design.

13:33.480 --> 13:35.820
It's called null convention logic.

13:36.180 --> 13:37.640
Null convention logic.

13:37.640 --> 13:38.200
Yeah.

13:38.700 --> 13:43.840
It basically provides a formal, rigorous way to distinguish between a computed value of zero

13:43.840 --> 13:46.600
and the sheer absence of a computation.

13:47.240 --> 13:47.460
Right.

13:47.520 --> 13:53.040
Because in standard programming, the distinction between zero and null is just notorious for causing database errors.

13:53.160 --> 13:53.400
Oh, yeah.

13:53.460 --> 13:54.020
It breaks everything.

13:54.700 --> 13:57.380
But in this context, it's not just a software bug.

13:57.480 --> 14:00.700
It fundamentally alters the weight of the global training route.

14:00.780 --> 14:02.800
Because they mean completely different things.

14:03.160 --> 14:06.880
A tensor filled with zeros is formally classified as data.

14:06.880 --> 14:08.520
Wait, zeros are data?

14:08.800 --> 14:09.060
Yes.

14:09.540 --> 14:12.320
It means a worker site received the global checkpoint,

14:12.940 --> 14:16.500
processed its local data set through the forward and backward passes,

14:16.820 --> 14:21.360
and mathematically concluded that the gradients for all parameters are exactly zero.

14:21.560 --> 14:22.480
No shift is required.

14:22.680 --> 14:22.900
Right.

14:23.140 --> 14:26.660
The local data perfectly aligns with the global model's current state.

14:26.940 --> 14:31.060
It is an active, computed decision to just remain stationary.

14:31.520 --> 14:31.820
Okay.

14:32.160 --> 14:35.380
But null represents an absolute lack of evidence.

14:35.440 --> 14:35.820
Exactly.

14:35.820 --> 14:38.240
The worker hasn't reported back at all.

14:38.820 --> 14:45.520
The node could be experiencing a hardware failure, a latency spike, or maybe a backhoe just severed the network connection.

14:45.880 --> 14:45.940
Right.

14:46.360 --> 14:49.740
The system has zero information about the state of that worker's gradient.

14:50.100 --> 14:50.540
Zero.

14:50.540 --> 14:55.760
Taking that to a real-world scenario, it's kind of like the difference between casting a ballot that says,

14:56.100 --> 14:58.740
I abstain, versus just staying home on election day.

14:58.940 --> 15:00.540
That's a perfect analogy, actually.

15:00.680 --> 15:00.820
Right.

15:00.880 --> 15:05.000
Because the zero tensor, the abstention, it counts toward the total voter turnout.

15:05.380 --> 15:09.120
It influences the denominator when you are calculating the weighted average of the round.

15:09.240 --> 15:09.520
Yes.

15:09.940 --> 15:12.000
But the null doesn't even exist in the denominator.

15:12.500 --> 15:12.960
Exactly.

15:12.960 --> 15:17.340
And that distinction drives the entire consensus mechanism for closing a training round.

15:17.880 --> 15:20.480
Flyxion outlines two operational modes for this.

15:21.240 --> 15:23.760
Input completeness and policy closure.

15:24.460 --> 15:24.500
Okay.

15:24.600 --> 15:25.720
What's input completeness?

15:26.380 --> 15:27.700
Input completeness is rigid.

15:27.700 --> 15:34.000
Like, a synchronization stage is only complete when every single expected input has arrived and transitioned from null to data.

15:34.620 --> 15:37.740
Which seems completely unworkable in a global WAN environment.

15:37.960 --> 15:42.220
Like, if you have 10,000 nodes waiting for the absolute slowest machine on a terrible connection,

15:42.560 --> 15:46.820
it means your multi-million dollar GPU cluster is just sitting idle, what, 90% of the time?

15:46.940 --> 15:47.360
Exactly.

15:47.700 --> 15:54.020
It's totally impractical, which is why practical systems rely on policy closure, often implemented as a quorum.

15:54.580 --> 15:55.080
A quorum.

15:55.300 --> 15:55.440
Yeah.

15:55.440 --> 15:58.460
The central root server basically evaluates a condition.

15:58.980 --> 16:05.280
Maybe a strict time limit has expired, or maybe a specific threshold of total data weight has been accumulated.

16:06.000 --> 16:09.920
Once that policy condition is met, the root unilaterally closes the round.

16:10.480 --> 16:16.080
And any node still registering as null at that specific millisecond is officially designated as absent.

16:16.320 --> 16:16.680
Yes.

16:17.180 --> 16:22.260
And what's fascinating here is how policy closure actually fractures the timeline of the network.

16:22.660 --> 16:24.060
Fractures the timeline.

16:24.060 --> 16:24.840
How so?

16:25.440 --> 16:28.520
Well, think about a delayed message from an absent worker.

16:29.060 --> 16:32.640
It might arrive at the central server two minutes after the quorum was already called.

16:32.880 --> 16:34.100
Oh, right, because of latency.

16:34.400 --> 16:34.580
Yeah.

16:34.860 --> 16:36.240
That late message is logged.

16:36.400 --> 16:39.300
It officially becomes part of the historical record of the system.

16:39.300 --> 16:42.080
Like, the node did eventually finish its work.

16:42.080 --> 16:48.500
But it is permanently excluded from the committed accepted set of that specific training round.

16:48.500 --> 16:49.940
Because the round was already closed.

16:49.940 --> 16:50.440
Right.

16:50.740 --> 16:51.360
Okay.

16:51.360 --> 16:56.140
So the accepted set is basically the immutable foundation for the next checkpoint.

16:56.140 --> 16:57.840
So let's recap.

16:57.840 --> 16:59.660
We've secured the foundation.

16:59.660 --> 17:05.000
We have tagged summaries preventing routing overlaps from warping the vectors.

17:05.000 --> 17:05.440
Yep.

17:05.440 --> 17:11.600
We have null convention logic strictly defining who is participating and who is absent based on quorum rules.

17:11.600 --> 17:15.880
So we have theoretically perfect records and perfectly executed math.

17:15.880 --> 17:17.460
In theory, yes.

17:18.080 --> 17:22.260
So the resulting AI model must be moving closer to its objective, right?

17:22.700 --> 17:26.140
Well, this is where the paper delivers its most severe warning.

17:26.400 --> 17:26.740
Oh, no.

17:26.740 --> 17:27.100
Yeah.

17:27.240 --> 17:28.840
This is the quadratic obstruction.

17:29.620 --> 17:34.720
Flyxion proves mathematically that even if the protocol layer is absolutely flawless,

17:35.020 --> 17:37.660
like no missing workers, no stale data, no double counting,

17:38.180 --> 17:41.420
the model can still systematically destroy its own performance.

17:41.820 --> 17:42.500
Wait, what?

17:43.180 --> 17:46.240
The math is perfectly executed, but the objective is lost anyway?

17:46.780 --> 17:47.240
Exactly.

17:47.640 --> 17:51.360
To illustrate this, the paper builds a two-site quadratic example.

17:51.780 --> 17:54.680
We basically have two remote sites, site one and site two,

17:54.680 --> 17:57.600
and they have completely different local data sets.

17:58.380 --> 18:02.140
Mathematically, we represent these local loss functions as parabolas.

18:02.920 --> 18:06.180
Let's try to visualize the loss landscape for the listeners.

18:06.700 --> 18:10.060
So a neural network is essentially trying to find the minimum of a function,

18:10.360 --> 18:13.460
the lowest point in the valley where the error is smallest, right?

18:13.780 --> 18:15.120
That's right, the bottom of the valley.

18:15.340 --> 18:17.760
But because site one and site two have different data,

18:18.220 --> 18:20.520
they are looking at two entirely different valleys.

18:20.860 --> 18:21.180
Yes.

18:21.180 --> 18:25.440
Site one's local function has a minimum at, let's say, coordinate U.

18:26.140 --> 18:28.860
Site two's local function has a minimum at coordinate V.

18:29.360 --> 18:32.640
And the steepness of those valleys is dictated by their curvature,

18:33.120 --> 18:35.620
which the paper defines as variables A and B.

18:36.300 --> 18:40.100
But the true global objective, like the model we actually want to build,

18:40.660 --> 18:46.060
is the exact mathematical minimum of the combined averaged function of both sites, right?

18:46.060 --> 18:50.080
Exactly. Now we have to introduce the variable H.

18:50.480 --> 18:52.840
H represents the number of local steps.

18:53.180 --> 18:54.380
This is the horizon.

18:54.720 --> 18:55.180
The horizon.

18:55.480 --> 19:01.660
It dictates how many sequential gradient descent updates a specific site executes on its local data

19:01.660 --> 19:05.520
before it is forced to communicate its shift back to the central server.

19:05.760 --> 19:10.640
So if H is one, they take a single step and immediately sync up with the server.

19:10.640 --> 19:15.140
Right. But to save on those massive communication costs we talked about,

19:15.640 --> 19:17.420
architects push H higher.

19:17.900 --> 19:23.000
They let the sites train independently for 50, 100, or even 500 steps.

19:23.460 --> 19:26.220
Right. Less syncing means less money spent on bandwidth.

19:26.820 --> 19:30.920
But as H increases, the system falls victim to client drift,

19:30.920 --> 19:35.540
which Flachian formally refers to as objective inconsistency.

19:36.000 --> 19:38.860
When sites train locally for an extended horizon,

19:39.260 --> 19:43.100
the effective weighting of their gradient steps actually saturates.

19:43.680 --> 19:46.120
Okay, let's break down exactly why it saturates,

19:46.220 --> 19:48.860
because it's not just a matter of walking too far, is it?

19:49.020 --> 19:49.820
No. No.

19:50.200 --> 19:52.640
The local gradient descent is an iterative process.

19:53.340 --> 19:56.220
With every single step, the model moves closer to the local minimum,

19:56.460 --> 19:58.800
which means the slope gets flatter, right?

19:58.800 --> 20:03.020
And so the subsequent gradient steps just become smaller and smaller.

20:03.760 --> 20:06.980
Exactly. The magnitude of the shift is decaying exponentially

20:06.980 --> 20:09.100
as it approaches that local fixed point.

20:09.300 --> 20:09.800
Oh, I see.

20:10.160 --> 20:12.040
And the mathematical proof in the paper demonstrates

20:12.040 --> 20:14.960
that taking a large number of local steps

20:14.960 --> 20:18.760
is not equivalent to taking one large step on the global gradient.

20:19.540 --> 20:23.460
The composite operator created by chaining H local steps together

20:23.460 --> 20:25.240
fundamentally warps the geometry.

20:25.820 --> 20:27.520
So it points in the wrong direction entirely.

20:27.920 --> 20:28.200
Yes.

20:28.200 --> 20:30.620
The global model converges to a fixed point

20:30.620 --> 20:34.380
that is mathematically distinct from the true global minimizer.

20:34.660 --> 20:34.780
Wow.

20:35.500 --> 20:38.700
It just stabilizes at a completely incorrect coordinate.

20:39.320 --> 20:41.080
And we really have to hammer this home for the listener.

20:41.420 --> 20:43.920
This is an exact mathematical obstruction.

20:44.620 --> 20:45.480
It is not a bug.

20:45.800 --> 20:46.080
Right.

20:46.300 --> 20:48.320
It is a property of the vector geometry.

20:48.960 --> 20:50.120
The system isn't breaking.

20:50.460 --> 20:52.340
It is functioning exactly as designed,

20:52.980 --> 20:54.740
calculating the gradients flawlessly,

20:55.060 --> 20:57.760
and arriving at a biased, objectively worse outcome.

20:57.760 --> 21:01.280
Just because the independent horizons altered the trajectory.

21:01.500 --> 21:04.720
Because you can't engineer your way out of a geometric reality.

21:04.980 --> 21:05.180
Yeah.

21:06.100 --> 21:08.500
Here's where it gets really interesting, I think.

21:09.460 --> 21:12.340
Think about a band practicing for a massive concert.

21:12.460 --> 21:12.700
Okay.

21:12.900 --> 21:13.780
Back to the analogies.

21:13.780 --> 21:18.960
If the drummer and the lead guitarist practice in their own separate soundproof rooms for 10 minutes,

21:19.340 --> 21:20.760
so a low H horizon,

21:21.400 --> 21:24.180
they can come back together, count off, and sync up perfectly.

21:24.300 --> 21:24.540
Sure.

21:24.540 --> 21:27.880
But if they practice in total isolation for a month,

21:28.360 --> 21:29.600
a massive H horizon,

21:30.160 --> 21:31.960
they aren't just playing the song anymore.

21:32.360 --> 21:35.060
They're developing entirely independent micro rhythms.

21:35.680 --> 21:36.740
Oh, I see where you're going with this.

21:36.840 --> 21:38.760
The drummer starts leaning slightly behind the beat.

21:39.200 --> 21:41.780
The guitarist starts pushing the tempo on the chorus.

21:42.520 --> 21:45.440
They are basically optimizing for their own isolated soundscapes.

21:45.680 --> 21:45.880
Right.

21:46.200 --> 21:48.880
And when you force them back together after a month,

21:49.100 --> 21:50.880
and perfectly combine their audio feeds,

21:50.920 --> 21:52.620
it just sounds like total chaos.

21:52.620 --> 21:53.100
Exactly.

21:53.680 --> 21:56.560
The resulting music is mathematically out of phase.

21:57.100 --> 21:59.480
They have drifted toward their own local minima.

21:59.840 --> 22:00.940
That's a great way to picture it.

22:01.300 --> 22:02.760
And what's really alarming is that

22:02.760 --> 22:06.800
throwing standard AI optimization tricks at this doesn't work at all.

22:07.140 --> 22:08.960
Like if the band sounds terrible,

22:09.260 --> 22:11.200
you can't just tell them to play faster or louder.

22:11.480 --> 22:11.740
Right.

22:11.840 --> 22:13.860
But that's exactly what people try to do with the math.

22:14.320 --> 22:16.420
The instinct is to apply outer momentum,

22:16.800 --> 22:18.220
like Nesterov momentum,

22:18.220 --> 22:21.500
or to just scale the outer learning rate on the central server

22:21.500 --> 22:23.700
to kind of force the model toward the target.

22:24.280 --> 22:24.900
Does that not work?

22:25.160 --> 22:25.320
No.

22:25.680 --> 22:28.480
Because momentum is just a rolling average of past gradients.

22:28.760 --> 22:31.860
It alters the speed and the oscillation of the convergence path,

22:31.980 --> 22:35.560
but it absolutely cannot move the fixed point itself.

22:35.840 --> 22:36.220
Wow.

22:36.640 --> 22:40.560
If the geometry of the H-step composite operator points to a biased coordinate,

22:41.020 --> 22:44.040
momentum just gets you to that incorrect coordinate faster.

22:44.240 --> 22:46.140
It's just accelerating towards failure.

22:46.140 --> 22:49.980
So if standard optimizers are completely blind to the drift,

22:50.460 --> 22:52.460
how does the field attempt to actually solve it?

22:52.860 --> 22:55.740
Well, the paper discusses control variate corrections,

22:56.320 --> 22:58.560
drawing parallels to algorithms like SCAFFOLD.

22:58.960 --> 22:59.520
SCAFFOLD.

22:59.660 --> 22:59.880
Okay.

23:00.220 --> 23:03.220
The concept is to basically provide each local site

23:03.220 --> 23:06.200
with a continuous mathematical correction term.

23:06.480 --> 23:09.240
Like a local compass that points to the global reality.

23:09.780 --> 23:12.880
So how do you actually calculate that correction term?

23:12.880 --> 23:16.080
The central server calculates the true global gradient

23:16.080 --> 23:18.900
and the exact local gradient at the starting checkpoint.

23:19.340 --> 23:22.500
The difference between those two vectors becomes the control variate.

23:23.060 --> 23:24.620
So it's basically a penalty term.

23:25.140 --> 23:25.540
Exactly.

23:26.000 --> 23:28.300
When the local site takes its independent steps,

23:28.460 --> 23:31.260
it adds this control variate to every single calculation,

23:31.880 --> 23:33.480
artificially tilting its local valley

23:33.480 --> 23:36.020
so that the bottom aligns more closely with the global valley.

23:36.560 --> 23:39.520
So it forces the drummer to hear like a ghost track of the guitarist

23:39.520 --> 23:40.420
while they practice alone.

23:40.580 --> 23:40.880
Yes.

23:40.880 --> 23:45.200
But Flyxion refers to this as an idealized solution

23:45.200 --> 23:49.360
because if we need the exact global gradient to fix the local drift,

23:49.460 --> 23:51.460
aren't we just like downloading this solution

23:51.460 --> 23:52.840
to figure out how to find the solution?

23:53.320 --> 23:54.780
That's exactly the paradox.

23:55.300 --> 23:58.120
The compupational and bandwidth costs of calculating

23:58.120 --> 24:00.860
and distributing that exact global gradient

24:00.860 --> 24:03.740
at the start of every single synchronization round

24:03.740 --> 24:04.740
are just exorbitant.

24:04.960 --> 24:05.160
Right.

24:05.160 --> 24:08.580
It requires a full pass over the entire global data set

24:08.580 --> 24:10.080
just to calibrate the compass.

24:10.640 --> 24:13.720
In a truly decentralized, massive scale system,

24:13.840 --> 24:16.900
the overhead of the correction destroys the communication savings

24:16.900 --> 24:19.400
you gained by isolating the workers in the first place.

24:19.400 --> 24:25.980
So we are just completely stuck between the physical limits of network bandwidth

24:25.980 --> 24:30.240
and the geometric limits of objective inconsistency.

24:30.720 --> 24:31.200
Precisely.

24:31.480 --> 24:35.080
Which is why Flyxion moves from the theoretical quadratics

24:35.080 --> 24:36.620
into a rigorous simulation

24:36.620 --> 24:41.160
to measure exactly how much this drift costs us in real world scenarios.

24:41.160 --> 24:42.160
Okay.

24:42.400 --> 24:43.620
Let's dig into that simulation.

24:44.120 --> 24:45.300
What was the setup?

24:46.040 --> 24:49.880
The simulation models a multilayer perceptron, or MLP,

24:50.520 --> 24:52.280
tasked with a classification problem.

24:52.880 --> 24:54.720
The architecture has 32 inputs,

24:55.340 --> 24:57.000
two hidden layers of width 128,

24:57.280 --> 24:58.700
and eight output nodes.

24:58.960 --> 25:02.040
And the training is distributed across two separate workers.

25:02.260 --> 25:02.640
Got it.

25:03.500 --> 25:05.540
And they test this architecture

25:05.540 --> 25:08.180
across two vastly different network environments, right?

25:08.440 --> 25:08.700
Right.

25:08.700 --> 25:11.040
A high-speed local area network environment

25:11.040 --> 25:12.780
representing a clustered supercomputer

25:12.780 --> 25:14.400
where latency is virtually zero.

25:14.680 --> 25:16.720
And then a wide area network environment

25:16.720 --> 25:19.800
representing the brutal realities of decentralized computing.

25:20.360 --> 25:22.080
So high latency, drop packets,

25:22.260 --> 25:23.620
and severe bandwidth constraints.

25:23.720 --> 25:24.140
Exactly.

25:24.800 --> 25:28.180
And the budget is capped at 3,000 local steps per worker

25:28.180 --> 25:30.800
to reach a specific target validation loss.

25:31.280 --> 25:33.260
Let's examine the raw economic incentive

25:33.260 --> 25:35.400
of the high H horizon first.

25:35.720 --> 25:37.720
Because on the slow high latency WAN link,

25:37.720 --> 25:39.980
forcing the workers to synchronize frequently,

25:40.380 --> 25:41.920
say, setting H to 50 steps,

25:42.060 --> 25:44.620
that requires constant back-and-forth communication.

25:44.760 --> 25:46.240
Yes, and the simulation shows

25:46.240 --> 25:49.600
it takes 12,306 modeled seconds

25:49.600 --> 25:51.100
to reach the target loss that way.

25:51.420 --> 25:51.640
Wow.

25:52.000 --> 25:52.260
Okay.

25:52.660 --> 25:54.780
But if you cut the communication overhead

25:54.780 --> 25:56.480
by letting the workers run independently

25:56.480 --> 25:58.860
for 500 steps before syncing...

25:58.860 --> 26:02.420
Then the time drops to just 2,226 seconds.

26:02.860 --> 26:05.020
That is a 5.5x speedup.

26:05.360 --> 26:07.040
The network savings are just immense.

26:07.040 --> 26:07.640
They're huge.

26:07.920 --> 26:09.560
You are slashing your cloud compute

26:09.560 --> 26:12.620
and your egress bandwidth bills by like 80%.

26:12.620 --> 26:14.700
But then the paper flips the script, right?

26:14.780 --> 26:17.860
By altering the statistical distribution of the data sets

26:17.860 --> 26:19.240
sitting on those two workers.

26:19.380 --> 26:19.640
Yes.

26:20.260 --> 26:24.680
They test mild heterogeneity versus strong heterogeneity.

26:24.880 --> 26:27.520
And heterogeneity basically defines

26:27.520 --> 26:29.640
how different the local data sets are

26:29.640 --> 26:30.760
from the global average.

26:30.880 --> 26:31.100
Right.

26:31.100 --> 26:33.200
Under mild heterogeneity,

26:33.420 --> 26:36.260
where the local data is largely identically distributed,

26:36.480 --> 26:37.880
just maybe slightly skewed,

26:38.400 --> 26:41.600
the long horizon of H equals 500 works beautifully.

26:41.600 --> 26:44.720
So the 5.5x speedup holds up.

26:44.840 --> 26:45.260
It does.

26:45.760 --> 26:47.960
And the model reaches the target validation loss

26:47.960 --> 26:49.120
without severe drift.

26:49.280 --> 26:51.060
Because the local valleys are close enough

26:51.060 --> 26:52.640
to the global valley

26:52.640 --> 26:54.500
that the model doesn't get completely lost.

26:54.500 --> 26:54.980
Exactly.

26:55.520 --> 26:58.440
But when they switch to strong heterogeneity,

26:58.580 --> 27:00.900
where the data sets are highly non-IID,

27:01.240 --> 27:03.480
meaning they look completely different from each other,

27:03.980 --> 27:05.820
the H equals 500 horizon

27:05.820 --> 27:07.960
completely detonates the model.

27:08.360 --> 27:09.020
Detonates it.

27:09.160 --> 27:09.740
Detonates it.

27:09.900 --> 27:12.060
So it doesn't just fail to reach the target loss

27:12.060 --> 27:12.940
in the allotted time.

27:13.100 --> 27:13.680
No, it's worse.

27:14.180 --> 27:16.260
The validation loss actually spikes.

27:17.060 --> 27:18.400
The model's performance degrades

27:18.400 --> 27:21.660
by over 172% compared to the baseline.

27:21.660 --> 27:24.100
So it actively unlearns.

27:24.420 --> 27:26.260
The parameters are just violently ripped apart

27:26.260 --> 27:27.660
by the divergent local gradients,

27:27.840 --> 27:29.820
and the model becomes catastrophically dumber

27:29.820 --> 27:31.080
the longer you let it train.

27:31.180 --> 27:31.800
It really does.

27:32.200 --> 27:34.520
And Flyxion runs the specific catastrophic failure

27:34.520 --> 27:36.780
using the Atom-W optimizer.

27:37.080 --> 27:38.000
Okay, Atom-W.

27:38.380 --> 27:40.960
Yeah, Atom-W is a highly sophisticated optimizer

27:40.960 --> 27:43.440
that maintains a running exponential moving average

27:43.440 --> 27:46.200
at both the first and second moments of the gradients.

27:46.200 --> 27:49.100
So it has a really heavy, persistent memory

27:49.100 --> 27:49.760
of where it has been.

27:50.320 --> 27:51.800
One could argue that the memory itself

27:51.800 --> 27:54.240
is actually dragging the model off course, right?

27:54.260 --> 27:56.420
Like the optimizer remembers the wrong valley

27:56.420 --> 27:57.640
and just refuses to leave.

27:58.400 --> 27:59.180
You could argue that,

27:59.600 --> 28:02.220
which is exactly why they rerun the entire simulation

28:02.220 --> 28:05.860
using standard, memoryless, stochastic gradient descent.

28:06.160 --> 28:06.640
SGD.

28:07.020 --> 28:07.280
Right.

28:07.760 --> 28:09.520
SGD doesn't track historical momentum.

28:09.720 --> 28:12.080
It just steps based on the immediate gradient

28:12.080 --> 28:13.020
of the current batch.

28:13.540 --> 28:14.260
And the result?

28:14.260 --> 28:16.960
SGD. The exact same catastrophic drift occurs

28:16.960 --> 28:18.180
under strong heterogeneity.

28:18.380 --> 28:18.760
SGD. Wow.

28:19.380 --> 28:21.240
So that isolates the variable completely.

28:21.640 --> 28:23.820
It proves definitively that the drift is geometric.

28:24.080 --> 28:26.200
It's caused by the interaction of the h-horizon

28:26.200 --> 28:28.220
and the disparate data distributions,

28:28.320 --> 28:31.200
not just some artifact of an optimizer's memory states.

28:31.540 --> 28:32.020
SGD. Yes.

28:32.760 --> 28:35.340
SGD. So you are basically looking at a system architecture

28:35.340 --> 28:38.340
that guarantees a 5.5x speedup

28:38.340 --> 28:39.820
if the data is similar,

28:39.820 --> 28:43.720
but guarantees a total catastrophic collapse of the model

28:43.720 --> 28:44.940
if the data is too different.

28:44.940 --> 28:46.360
SGD. Basically, yeah.

28:46.800 --> 28:47.840
SGD. And as a systems engineer,

28:47.980 --> 28:50.720
you have no visibility into the statistical distribution

28:50.720 --> 28:52.120
of the remote edge devices.

28:52.620 --> 28:55.040
So how do you mathematically detect

28:55.040 --> 28:56.560
if your model is learning

28:56.560 --> 28:58.280
or if it is currently in the process

28:58.280 --> 28:59.600
of completely unlearning?

28:59.600 --> 29:00.980
SGD. The paper proposes

29:00.980 --> 29:03.220
a critical diagnostic tool for this,

29:03.580 --> 29:05.520
calculating the mean local shift

29:05.520 --> 29:06.740
at a reference solution.

29:06.940 --> 29:07.300
SGD. Okay.

29:07.660 --> 29:09.640
Walk us through the mechanics of that calculation.

29:09.780 --> 29:09.920
SGD. Sure.

29:10.460 --> 29:12.700
So instead of looking at the aggregate shift

29:12.700 --> 29:13.900
at the end of the round,

29:14.440 --> 29:15.240
you freeze the model

29:15.240 --> 29:17.760
at a specific central reference parameter,

29:18.020 --> 29:19.420
usually just the starting checkpoint.

29:19.620 --> 29:20.500
SGD. Okay. Freeze it there.

29:20.720 --> 29:22.200
SGD. Then you instruct the local nodes

29:22.200 --> 29:23.400
to evaluate the gradient

29:23.400 --> 29:25.020
of their local loss functions

29:25.020 --> 29:26.820
at that exact frozen coordinate

29:26.820 --> 29:28.800
without taking any steps.

29:28.800 --> 29:29.500
SGD. Got it.

29:29.560 --> 29:31.920
So you are basically taking a snapshot

29:31.920 --> 29:33.500
of the slope at the starting line.

29:33.560 --> 29:33.840
SGD. Exactly.

29:34.600 --> 29:36.240
You average those local gradients

29:36.240 --> 29:37.820
to find the true global gradient

29:37.820 --> 29:38.940
at that exact point.

29:39.640 --> 29:41.480
SGD. Then you let the local nodes

